{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the bug clearly. Let me look at how `image->matte` is set, and how the opacity channel gets uninitialized.\n\nIn the RGBAStrippedMethod, when `image->matte` is true, `q->opacity` is set. When false, opacity is not set. But the resize code reads `p[j].opacity` when `source->matte` is true.\n\nSo the key: `image->matte` must be false during read (so opacity not initialized), but `pyramid_image->matte` must be true during resize/write. Hmm, but pyramid_image is a CloneImage of image... \n\nActually wait. Let me re-read the description: \"the opacity channel is not assured to be initialized in the RGBAStrippedMethod case.\" So perhaps `image->matte` is true in the read but something about the SetImagePixels cache — when the pixel cache is opened via ModifyCache and pixels are set with SetCacheViewPixels, the opacity channel might not be written because... Hmm.\n\nActually, look at error: `SetCacheViewPixels` at tiff.c:2994 — that's in RGBAStrippedMethod (the SyncImagePixels call). The uninit memory is from OpenCache via MagickRealloc. So the pixel cache memory is malloc'd and not zeroed. The pixels' opacity fields are uninitialized. But if image->matte is true, q->opacity is written... unless the image has no matte.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe the case is: `image->matte` is false during read. So opacity never set -> uninit. Then later, during write (WritePTIFImage), `pyramid_image = CloneImage(image, ...)`. CloneImage preserves matte=false. So resize uses the non-matte branch which sets opacity=OpaqueOpacity. So no UMR. Contradiction.\n\nUnless... the image->matte changes. Let me look at where matte gets set. In ReadTIFFImage, there's code that sets image->matte based on extrasamples. Let me search.\n\nActually maybe the trick is different: the cache is opened with columns*rows but SetImagePixels(image,0,y,columns,1) sets a region. When SyncImagePixels, the pixels are synced