{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"reasoning","text":"The poc file has an IFD at offset 12 with 288 entries, but the entries are mostly garbage 0x2020 spaces. Only a couple of valid tags (ImageWidth 0x100 = 256, BitsPerSample 0x101 = 8). This is a malformed TIFF where libtiff parses 288 \"directory entries\" mostly from spaces. Interesting - the poc is 81987 bytes.\n\nActually this poc is likely a fuzzer-generated file. The vulnerability: image width 256, bits per sample 8. The MSAN trace says RGBAStrippedMethod at tiff.c:2994 → SetImagePixels, and opacity uninitialized. So the image has matte=True but RGBAStrippedMethod doesn't set opacity when... wait, it does set q->opacity if image->matte. Unless image->matte is false during read but true later? Actually the trace says the uninitialized value created by the heap alloc in SetImagePixels (the pixel cache), and used in VerticalFilter. \n\nHmm, actually the bug: In RGBAStrippedMethod, opacity is set only `if (image->matte)`. If image->matte is false, opacity is left uninitialized in the pixel cache. Then later, when writing PTIF, the image is resized. In VerticalFilter, it checks `if ((source->matte) || (source->colorspace == CMYKColorspace))`. So matte must be true at resize time. So somewhere between read and write, matte got set to true. OR the RGBAStrippedMethod path is reached while matte is true but the specific `if` didn't execute... \n\nWait, more carefully: the code reads `if (image->matte) q->opacity=...`. If image->matte is true, it sets opacity. So how is opacity uninit? Unless image->matte was false during reading but the pixel cache still got opacity bytes allocated as uninitialized, and then matte set true later. Let me check where matte is set: line 2063, 2089. Let me look."}}
