{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"reasoning","text":"Now let's understand the PoC. The poc is crafted TIFF with 288 IFD entries. The poc file has \"II*\" and offset 12, but the first entry at offset 12 starts with tag 0x100 (ImageWidth), then the next bytes are 0x2020... which look like padding. Wait, entry 0: tag 0x100 type 0x10 count 1 val 0x125. Actually the bytes at offset 12: `20 01 00 01 10 00 01 00 00 00 25 01 00 00` = tag 0x0120? Let me re-read: bytes are \"20 01 00 01 10 00 01 00 00 00 25 01 00 00 20 20\". So tag = 0x0120 (288)? No: little-endian u16 = 0x0120 = 288. Then type=0x0100? Hmm.\n\nWait the parse gave tag 0x100 because it read bytes as `00 01`. Let me recount. IFD offset = 12. d[12:14] = bytes. From od: offset 0x0c = `20 01`? Let's check: line 000000: `49 49 2a 00 0c 00 00 00 20 20 20 20 20 01 00 01` positions: 0:49 1:49 2:2a 3:00 4:0c 5:00 6:00 7:00 8:20 9:20 10:20 11:20 12:20 13:01 14:00 15:01.\n\nSo d[12:14] = 0x0120 = 288. d[14:16]=0x0100=256. Hmm the count is 288 at offset 12? So the IFD at offset 12 has count 288 (0x120). Then entries start at offset 14. Entry 0: d[14:26] = `00 01 10 00 01 00 00 00 25 01 00 00`. tag=0x0100 (256 ImageWidth), type=0x0010=16? Hmm type 16 is invalid (LONG=4). Wait type bytes d[16:18]=0x0010? Let me index: 14:00 15:01 => tag 0x0100. 16:10 17:00 => type 0x0010 = 16. Hmm type 16 is invalid TIFF type (max 12). count d[18:22]=01 00 00 00 =1. value d[22:26]=25 01 00 00 = 0x125 = 293. So width=293. Type 16? Actually TIFF type 16 is... no. Hmm but maybe the parser reads it differently. Anyway.\n\nThis is a fuzzer-generated TIFF. The key: rows=293, and RGBAStrippedMethod used.\n\nLet me just create my own TIFF instead of reverse engineering this messy one. I'll write a clean TIFF generator.\n\nNow, plan for the actual exploit:\n\nThe vulnerability: In RGBAStrippedMethod, when image->matte is true but TIFFReadRGBAStrip returns a raster where the alpha channel (A) is not valid... Actually the MSan rep